---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import walkaroundHifi from '../../assets/work/cat-inspect/walkaround-hifi.png';
import dashboardHifi from '../../assets/work/cat-inspect/dashboard-hifi.png';

const sections = [
  {
    id: 'problem',
    navLabel: 'Problem',
    heading: 'Revisiting an earlier version of this app surfaced how much friction still lived in dense manual checklists.',
    body: [
      'Cat Inspect is a field tool for technicians, service engineers, and fleet managers running daily walk-arounds, preventive maintenance checks, and diagnostic visual assessments on heavy equipment. I had designed an earlier version of this app before; going back to it during HackIllinois 2026 with fresh eyes made the gaps obvious. Multi-step paper-style forms demanded repetitive manual entry in outdoor, gloved-hand conditions. Diagnostic notes varied wildly by technician experience, and nothing in the flow helped a tech prioritize what to check first when time and connectivity were both limited.',
    ],
    pullQuote: '“The first version worked. It just didn’t respect the fact that someone would be using it one-handed, in the rain, with a machine idling next to them.”',
  },
  {
    id: 'process',
    navLabel: 'Process',
    heading: 'Rebuilding the flow around field conditions first, AI assistance second.',
    body: [
      'Working with a three-person team at HackIllinois 2026, I led the design direction: re-examining the original inspection flow against the actual constraints of field work — gloved hands, bright outdoor light, intermittent connectivity, and technicians at very different experience levels. Rather than starting from the AI features, I started from the checklist itself: what has to happen in what order, what can be inferred automatically, and what a technician should never have to type twice.',
      'From there, we scoped which AI-assisted pieces were worth prototyping in the time we had. Computer vision defect detection and voice-to-text diagnostic annotation both got working prototypes; predictive maintenance suggestions and fully adaptive checklist sequencing stayed at the design-concept stage — real ideas in the flow, not features we could demo.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'decision',
    navLabel: 'Decision',
    heading: 'One structured entry point, then a guided step-by-step walk-around instead of a flat form.',
    body: [
      'The redesign splits the experience in two. A single asset dashboard replaces free-form intake: QR-scan auto-fill for asset ID, an explicit "Synced" state so offline work is never ambiguous, and pre-inspection steps that block progress until required fields are actually filled — no more submitting an inspection with silently missing data.',
      'From there, the walk-around itself became a guided, one-item-at-a-time flow: large high-contrast touch targets sized for gloves, a color-coded status selector (Normal / Monitor / Action Required / N/A) instead of free text, and hands-free capture built directly into each step — hold to speak a note, or attach a photo without leaving the checklist. The computer-vision prototype pre-fills a draft severity rating from that photo for the technician to confirm or override, rather than deciding for them.',
    ],
    pullQuote: '“Every AI suggestion in the flow is a draft the technician confirms, never an answer they have to trust blind.”',
    images: [
      {
        src: dashboardHifi,
        alt: 'Daily Inspection dashboard for a CAT D8T Dozer showing sync status, a pre-inspection setup checklist with QR auto-fill, and walk-around progress',
        caption: 'The redesigned entry point: structured pre-inspection steps and explicit offline-sync state, before the walk-around begins.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'outcome',
    navLabel: 'Outcome',
    heading: 'A rebuilt, AI-assisted inspection flow — untested outside the team so far.',
    body: [
      'By the end of HackIllinois 2026, the redesigned flow existed as a high-fidelity interactive prototype: the guided walk-around, the restructured asset dashboard, and working prototypes of computer-vision defect detection and voice-to-text annotation.',
      'We didn’t end up presenting it at the event, so there’s no judge or user feedback to report yet — this is a personal redesign iteration, not a validated result. Predictive maintenance suggestions and fully adaptive checklist ordering remain concepts in the design, not built.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Cat Inspect"
  dek="Redesigning Caterpillar's field equipment inspection app around glove-friendly, AI-assisted workflows for technicians working outdoors with limited connectivity."
  role="Lead Product Designer"
  timeline="HackIllinois 2026"
  team="3-person team"
  skills="Field UX Design · AI-Integrated Workflows · Rapid Prototyping · Accessibility (High-Contrast, Glove-Friendly)"
  gradient="coral"
  heroImage={walkaroundHifi}
  heroImageAlt="Guided walk-around inspection step for a CAT D8T Dozer showing a fluid systems check with a color-coded status selector and voice/photo capture"
  sections={sections}
/>
